ARCHITECTURE NOTE (honesty): the web source is an UNROUTED, purely presentational fragment the Automation builder renders
inline (single prop: `conflicts: AutomationConflict[]`; no data hook — its only `t()` call is the banner title). It maps the
conflicts to a `space-y-2` stack of inline `<AlertBanner>`s (warning tone + triangle glyph when `severity === 'warning'`,
info tone + info glyph otherwise; title "Potential Conflict"; body `"${automation_name}": ${reason}`) and returns null when the
list is empty. The presentational + projection surface was already implemented at parity by the predecessor prompt
p3-android/feature-views ConflictWarnings (com/teslasync/feature-views/ConflictWarnings, committed + green; fv unit tests
ConflictWarningsProjectionTest / ConflictWarningsDiagnosticsTest + UI test ConflictWarningsUiTest already in the suite). This
page prompt targets the separate allowed-files path kotlin/com/teslasync/automations/** with a `@Composable screen + ViewModel`
contract, so — following the sanctioned AutomationCard / ConditionBuilder precedent — this layer is a THIN page wrapper that
EMBEDS that one shared surface (every banner + the parity string is the parity-covered feature view's `ConflictWarningsContent`)
and adds only the page state holder + the stateless screen. Predecessor NOT bypassed; no rendering re-implemented.

DATA-SOURCE NOTE (honesty): the manifest declares NO API data source — "renders from navigation args / local state". The host
(the Automation builder, which owns the validate/preview query) supplies the `conflicts` array. ConflictWarningsPageViewModel
carries no networking and performs no HTTP; it only re-presents the host-supplied conflicts onto a single
StateFlow<UiState<List<AutomationConflict>>> (Content when present, Empty when none — web `conflicts.length === 0`). The two
data states the manifest declares for this unit (empty · error) are rendered by the stateless ConflictWarningsScreen as the
standard chrome around the embedded fv content; no error is fabricated in production (with no data source there is nothing to
fail — the holder only ever resolves Content/Empty, while the Error branch stays reachable through the same StateFlow type).

NAV NOTE (honesty): web route is "(unrouted)". Destinations.kt is the canonical registry — it records only real web routes and
RouteTableTest round-trips every Destinations.all entry, so an unrouted presentational fragment fabricates NO synthetic nav
destination (doing so would violate the registry's anti-fabrication contract + the round-trip test). The page layer is embedded
by the Automation builder exactly as the feature view is — the prompt's "(if routed)" clause does not apply, identical to the
sibling AutomationCard / ConditionBuilder pages.

GATE PATH NOTE: the project Gradle path is ':android' (settings.gradle.kts maps ':android' -> app/); the prompt snippet's
':app:' is the same module (documented alias, as in every sibling P3/A* gate log).

DETEKT NOTE (honesty): detekt is NOT part of the required android triad gate (placeholder + assembleDebug + lintDebug +
testDebugUnitTest). The optional :android:detektDebug task reports 498 pre-existing, ungated findings repo-wide; the only one
